## What landed

tests/global_state_races.rs — a fifth gate in the same shape as the four existing ones, plus a CLAUDE.md bullet beside them.

The measurement, which was the point:

```
5,113 tests scanned in src/
   98 call a global setter directly
   81 already carry #[serial]
   17 do not  ← the register
```

17 named tests across 6 files (format/mod.rs 5, commands_session.rs 4, cli_config.rs 3, commands_run.rs 3, conversations.rs 1, format/tools.rs 1). That's 83% compliance — considerably better than Phase A1's crude ~74-site upper bound, which over-counted because it read a fixed byte window past each fn and bled into neighbouring tests. I brace-match instead, over a scanner that skips comments, char literals (a lifetime tick is not a char literal), and normal + raw strings.

Shape: pure classify(tests, register) with all filesystem walking at one call site; fatal on unserialised-and-unregistered with all three remedies verbatim; fatal ratchet when a registered test gains #[serial], stops calling a setter, or vanishes; key is (path, name), pinned by test. GLOBAL_SETTERS (40 names) is hand-written and tied to reality by a second test that fails on a rename.

**Verified by breaking it, not by reading it:**
• anti-vacuous check → fires ("no test in src/ was seen calling any of the 1 names")
• drift guard → fires (`"GLOBAL_SETTERS names 1 function(s) that no longer exist"`)
• three limits print in a plain cargo test, no --nocapture — the raw-stderr trick, because libtest discards macro output from passing tests

Fixed nothing. No #[serial] added, no test rewritten, git diff --stat shows zero src/ files. The deliverable is the dial.

The limit I want to keep loud: #[serial] only serialises against other #[serial] tests, so a test this gate calls compliant is still exposed to unmarked *readers* of the same global. #780's actual victims were unmarked CWD-readers — every one of them would have passed this gate. Compliance here means "follows the repo convention", not "proven race-free".
